@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    TST-71  The provider spec declares its fixtures inline: one at the
            top, and one somebody appended at the bottom of the file.
            Move both into order.fixture.ts as named exports and
            import them into the spec.

Three buffers are open. `:bn` and `:bp` move between them.

  1. order.provider.spec.ts — the `pendingOrder` and `shippedOrder`
     objects leave this file entirely, blank lines included. The
     `import { Order }` line is no longer needed and becomes
     `import { pendingOrder, shippedOrder } from './order.fixture';`
     in the same place.

  2. order.fixture.ts — already has the `Order` import. Below it, a
     blank line, then the two fixtures in the same order, each with
     `export ` in front of `const`, separated by one blank line.

  3. order.entity.ts — open for reference only. Leave it alone.

Both fixtures should travel in one register and land in one put.

@keys
| `"adap` | delete a paragraph into `a` |
| `"AdG` | append everything to the end of the file |
| `"ap` | put the collection |
| `ci{` | change inside the braces |
| `I` then `.` | insert at the start of the line, repeat it on another |
| `n` | the last search survives a buffer switch |
| `:bn` `:bp` `:wqa` | move between buffers, write and quit everything |

@hints
Fix the import line first, while you are at the top of the spec. `ci{`
on the `import { Order }` line replaces what is between the braces;
`ciw` on `entity` finishes it.
--
The top fixture is a paragraph with a blank line after it, so `"adap`
takes both. The bottom fixture runs to the end of the file, so `"AdG`
from its first line appends it and the blank line above it.
--
In the fixture file, `"ap` puts both blocks below the import. `O` `Esc`
above the first block gives the blank line. Then `Iexport ` `Esc` on the
first `const`. Your last search was `const shipped` and it still is, so
`n` finds the second block and `.` repeats the insert.
--
`:wqa` writes every buffer and quits. Nothing else needs to happen to
the entity.

@solution
One route, buffer by buffer.

**order.provider.spec.ts** — `/Order }` Enter, `ci{`, type
` pendingOrder, shippedOrder ` and `Esc`. `/entity` Enter, `ciwfixture`,
`Esc`. Then `/const pending` Enter, `"adap`. `/const shipped` Enter,
`"AdG`.

**order.fixture.ts** — `:bn`, `"ap`, `O` `Esc`, `j`, `Iexport ` `Esc`,
`n`, `.`. `:wqa`.

The register did the carrying. Two blocks from opposite ends of one
file, one put in another, and nothing was retyped except the word
`export`, which the dot command typed the second time. Without the
capital-letter append this is two round trips between buffers; without
a named register at all it is two round trips with the unnamed register
one stray `dd` away from losing a fixture.

Notice `"AdG` rather than a second `dap`. The last paragraph of a file
has no blank line after it, so `ap` reaches up and takes the one before
instead. `dG` is honest about what it deletes: everything from here to
the end.

@start order.provider.spec.ts
import { stub, reveal } from 'jest-auto-stub';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

const pendingOrder: Order = {
  id: 'ord_001',
  customerId: 'cus_042',
  status: 'PENDING',
  total: 1999,
};

describe('OrderProvider', () => {
  const orderRepository = stub<OrderRepository>();
  const paymentService = stub<PaymentService>();
  const provider = new OrderProvider(orderRepository, paymentService);

  it('cancels a pending order', async () => {
    reveal(orderRepository).findOne.mockResolvedValue(pendingOrder);

    await provider.cancel(pendingOrder.id);

    expect(reveal(paymentService).refund).toHaveBeenCalledWith(pendingOrder.id);
  });

  it('refuses to cancel a shipped order', async () => {
    reveal(orderRepository).findOne.mockResolvedValue(shippedOrder);

    await expect(provider.cancel(shippedOrder.id)).rejects.toThrow('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
  });
});

const shippedOrder: Order = {
  id: 'ord_002',
  customerId: 'cus_042',
  status: 'SHIPPED',
  total: 4500,
};

@expected order.provider.spec.ts
import { stub, reveal } from 'jest-auto-stub';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { pendingOrder, shippedOrder } from './order.fixture';

describe('OrderProvider', () => {
  const orderRepository = stub<OrderRepository>();
  const paymentService = stub<PaymentService>();
  const provider = new OrderProvider(orderRepository, paymentService);

  it('cancels a pending order', async () => {
    reveal(orderRepository).findOne.mockResolvedValue(pendingOrder);

    await provider.cancel(pendingOrder.id);

    expect(reveal(paymentService).refund).toHaveBeenCalledWith(pendingOrder.id);
  });

  it('refuses to cancel a shipped order', async () => {
    reveal(orderRepository).findOne.mockResolvedValue(shippedOrder);

    await expect(provider.cancel(shippedOrder.id)).rejects.toThrow('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
  });
});

@start order.fixture.ts
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@expected order.fixture.ts
import { Order } from './order.entity';

export const pendingOrder: Order = {
  id: 'ord_001',
  customerId: 'cus_042',
  status: 'PENDING',
  total: 1999,
};

export const shippedOrder: Order = {
  id: 'ord_002',
  customerId: 'cus_042',
  status: 'SHIPPED',
  total: 4500,
};

@start order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'SETTLED' | 'CANCELLED';
